---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE_FULL_NAME, SITE_NAME } from "../config";
---
<Base title="About" breadcrumb={[{ text: "About" }]}>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="summary">
    {SITE_FULL_NAME}: the code published by the authors of open-access neuroscience papers, found in the paper and
    verified at the source.
  </p>
  <p>
    For every open-access neuroscience paper, a harvester looks for where its authors published their code: in the
    text of the paper, in its metadata, on forges and archives. It then verifies that code at the source: does the
    link answer, what is in it, under which license?
  </p>

  <h2>The tracing map</h2>
  <p>
    Every paper of the catalogue has a tracing map: the links between the paper and the repository of its code
    (commit, license, files). The harvester proposes it; an author of the paper can validate or correct it by
    signing in with their ORCID.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Only a map validated by an author receives a DOI, on Zenodo. A map proposed by the machine never receives one.</li>
    <li>
      The DOI is on the map (its links and metadata), never on the code: the code stays in its authors' repository,
      which the map references. {SITE_NAME} never deposits the code again.
    </li>
    <li>
      The map is linked to the paper (<code>IsSupplementTo</code>) and to the repository of the code
      (<code>References</code>). Its creators are the author who validated it and the platform.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is published, and what is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Neither the text nor the PDF of a paper: a DOI link. Only its abstract and its code and data availability
      statements are shown, and only when the paper's license allows it (CC BY, CC0, CC BY-SA, CC BY-NC); otherwise the
      page says what the statements point to, in a few facts, and links to the paper.
    </li>
    <li>
      The text of a script only when the license of its repository allows republishing it. Otherwise, the list of
      files links each of them to the source, at the verified commit.
    </li>
    <li>The source always prevails.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Levels of evidence</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>found</dt><dd>the paper cites the link</dd>
    <dt>alive</dt><dd>the link answers</dd>
    <dt>inventoried</dt><dd>the files are listed, the scripts counted, the commit recorded</dd>
    <dt>imported</dt><dd>a copy is kept, when the license allows it</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>How Code ↔ Paper works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The paper is loaded from Europe PMC (open access) by your browser, when you open the reader. {SITE_NAME} never stores
      it and never serves it: without Europe PMC, the reader only offers links to the paper.
    </li>
    <li>
      Its paragraphs are numbered in the order of its XML full text. The matches between a paragraph and lines of
      code are computed by the harvester beforehand, from that same numbering; each shows its evidence (a few
      short technical terms, never sentences of the paper), its score, and the method that produced it. They are
      proposals, not statements validated by the authors.
    </li>
    <li>
      The code shown is the text of the repository at the verified commit, when its license allows republishing it;
      otherwise the reader links to the file, and to the exact lines, at the source.
    </li>
    <li>
      A pair has one color on both sides. Click a highlighted paragraph to see its lines of code, or highlighted lines
      to see their paragraph; the list of matches above the panes does both.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>
